{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 454b5a2f97cb1e0c1ed536975a209c5cfbe7ddb1. This head touches no card path, so no citation is owed. I tried to get a forged register to `consistent` by reading, and I could not. The rule holds for the reasons below, and it does not show that another room exists as stated, which the report says.\n\n**`#otherRoomResult`: tight.** It needs all of these: the entry under judgment delivers exactly this fact, as an `applied` result; the fact is entry 0 of a directory that is not the reader's own; that entry is on the register, which is the scope that this run opened; the request that the result names sits at that sequence with that hash on the sealed register; and that request's send names a seed whose scope ID is this directory. The service must also answer `forbidden`. A seed's scope ID derives from its fields, so a server cannot point a real request at a chosen directory without changing the signed register entry.\n\n**Where I pressed.**\n- **The other callers of `#prove`.** Only the call at `:2068` passes `entry`. The calls at `:1683`, `:2005` and `:2013` pass none, so the rule cannot fire on them.\n- **The cache.** I looked for a second use of a cached anchor that skips the rule. `#proven` holds \"anchored\" for this fact, but line 1548 honours a cached anchor only through `#anchor`, which reads the caller's own anchors. So a second user of that fact falls through, opens the scope again, and must pass the rule on its own. No bypass there.\n- **The reader's own directory** is excluded by scope ID, from the local config and not from the server.\n- **Whether an anchor can change this room's state.** An anchored result moves another room's `claim` on the register to `active`. Your room's scopes read nothing from another room's claim. The cut of the register's end, which your first forgery needed, is the existing trust in the head, and the report already says that.\n\n**Condition 1, \"give that test a control\": met, with your caveat stated.** Removing the seed check moves the answer from `not proven` to `mismatch`. That shows that the test tells the two apart, and that the rule stops the forgery first. It cannot show a forgery passing, because the replay derives the register's entry again, even with the source check removed too. That is a stronger position than one guard with a control that reaches `consistent`, and the test still fails if the rule weakens. So I read it as meeting the planner's condition. The planner may read it otherwise, and your note gives it what it needs to decide.\n\n**Condition 2: met.** The second line, the heading, the trust and the last line of `--all` all say it. A first room's report has none of these words, and a test holds that. The line is absent on any other answer, which is right, because it describes what a consistent result covers.\n\n**Condition 3: met, with one difference for the planner.** I read both `--all` logs. They are two runs: the same result, with different byte counts and times in `rita.err` and `una.err`, so the matching digests come from matching results. `claim.scope.test.ts` flips. `only an actual Session-forbidden Directory genesis` stays as it was, because its fixture refuses the reader's own directory, and `not proven` stays the right answer there. That test now holds the rule that an anchor never covers the own directory. Keeping it is the right call, and it differs from the planner's \"flip the two\", so the planner should see it. Your request says so.\n\n**Limits, agreed.** A server can invent a room that no one reads. The report lists and counts those results, and it does not claim that they exist as stated. A person has not read these words.\n\n**Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` is 9b44c090…, every digest verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's `head.txt` is 8d5acf45, exit 0, with 3540 and 1022 tests. The failed first gate is kept and named. The merge of main touches no file of this head. I ran `verify.test.ts` (8 passed), `claim.scope.test.ts` (2 passed), `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (1002, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (3, 0 flagged). The worktree is clean. I did not rerun the controls or the `--all` runs, which were read-only on dap-demo.\n\n**Architecture.** One option, one rule, one new trust. The option is off unless the caller names its own directory, so every other caller of `verify` behaves as before. Section 9.4's rule, that a retained copy does not show that the source admitted the entry, stays stated through that trust line.\n\n**Security.** This widens what a verifier accepts, and only by a trust it names in words. It keeps each room's reads closed, which is the point of the planner's option. A reader who wants more than this can compare the count over time, since a growing count on a register is itself visible.\n\n**Simplification.** Nothing to remove.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cbbf653d4f3cc7fdfdcb31e7052d37e19997e418","binding":"assigned","head":"454b5a2f97cb1e0c1ed536975a209c5cfbe7ddb1","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cefa16bea9181912913fb5f1dc946ff63ff53e58\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7e6ce13bb357e549345d6236b51809d84e72f563\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cefa16bea9181912913fb5f1dc946ff63ff53e58","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}